  "image_name": "be9427de67415777faba67dd89d7687a.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the tectonic evolution of the India–Eurasia collision zone from about 65 Ma to the present. Across four time slices, it shows India moving northward beneath Eurasia, the progressive closure of the Neo‑Tethyan ocean, and the birth of the Himalaya and southern Tibetan basins.\n\nEarly on, a marine foreland basin lay between India and Eurasia, while the Neo‑Tethyan oceanic lithosphere subducted beneath Eurasia, feeding magmatism in the Gangdese arc. As collision intensified, sediments accumulated in basins such as Gamba Dingri and Sangdanlin, and thrusting and folding migrated southward. One panel highlights break‑off of the subducted oceanic slab, after which marine deposits give way to more continental clastic units and flysch, indicating uplift and erosion of the growing mountain belt.\n\nBy the youngest stage, the landscape has changed to a high Himalaya with a foreland basin on the Indian side and internal Tibetan basins farther north. Major structures including the MFT, MBT, MCT, and STDS are shown as stacked thrust systems accommodating crustal shortening and exhumation. The final panel also suggests crustal melting and uplift, with leucogranite and high‑pressure rocks, plus delamination of Indian lithosphere beneath the range.",
